Dear Fabbaloo Editors,

Solidon3D 0.3.1 has been released. The local desktop application started as a
way to adapt downloaded 3D-printing models without rebuilding them in CAD. The
new release closes the obvious gap in that idea: a recognised feature is no
longer only resizable, it is editable.

An imported mesh carries no design history — it carries triangles. Solidon
recognises holes, pegs, countersinks and domes in it, and from 0.3.1 they can
be moved, rotated, resized, duplicated and removed. The identity survives the
edit, which is the part that matters for a workflow: filling a hole and
drilling a new one yields the same shape but a new feature, and every fit
pointing at the old one is orphaned.

Three additions that show how the workflow has grown:

* a dedicated feature panel presents the measured values as editable fields
  with a live preview, so a changed number is the operation — no dialog in
  between;
* one checkbox applies an edit to every feature of the same kind, so a row of
  holes becomes a single transaction with a single undo;
* recognition is now limited to what a printer can make, which removed 296 of
  1130 tree entries on a downloaded hose holder — most of them fillets of
  effectively zero radius.

The release also replaces the default view navigation (left pans, right
orbits, W/A/S/D flies, with the familiar slicer and CAD schemes still
selectable) and refuses broken files at open time instead of accepting them
into the project.

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Release history: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

I can send a compact story pack with the 0.3.1 build, a measured feature edit,
